{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-181172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044982/assets/5c4ffbb64a754df9a6100fda3fd6eaf4a68f28ac59de4dfb716c2c8b1f2847ab.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,{x}^{2}}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044982/state.json" "file_sha256" "c513381e386b074cae64cf800a2b6e4ed1a1ffbb396bbffa819854727f89417b"} {"question" "q-181172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The points in our set include all those that lie *below* the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $y={x}^{2}$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\ny\\le {x}^{2}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\le {x}^{2}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044982/state.json" "file_sha256" "c513381e386b074cae64cf800a2b6e4ed1a1ffbb396bbffa819854727f89417b"} {"question" "q-181172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044982/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fd7aec7b89ddd4a554de8851428d4e55bf1012990a97ac62d7a942bcacf35152"} {"question" "q-181172" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer