c9d595a45dc64fbdfc6b1c83c152fb6fc892373ea06.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,-1}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044982/state.json" "file_sha256" "c513381e386b074cae64cf800a2b6e4ed1a1ffbb396bbffa819854727f89417b"} {"question" "q-182016" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in Z,y\\in Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in Z\\times Z={Z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-1.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-1\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that some points in our set lie *on* the line. We include these points by changing the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\ny\\ge -1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {Z}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge -1}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044982/state.json" "file_sha256" "c513381e386b074cae64cf800a2b6e4ed1a1ffbb396bbffa819854727f89417b"} {"question" "q-182016" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044982/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fd7aec7b89ddd4a554de8851428d4e55bf1012990a97ac62d7a942bcacf35152"} {"question" "q-182016" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044982/state.json" "file_sha256" "c513381e386b074cae64cf800a2b6e4ed1a1ffbb396bbffa819854727f89417b"} {"question" "q-182016" "field" "field-1" "attribut